            <ArticleTitle>Isolated left ventricle diverticula: a possible cause of atypical angina?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>W17-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/RTI.0b013e3181b5d833</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old woman with atypical chest pain was referred for a multidetector computed tomography examination of the coronary arteries after a positive electrocardiography stress test. No coronary atherosclerotic disease was observed on the coronary computed tomography scan. The only findings were 2 side-by-side basal left ventricle diverticula, suggested as a possible etiology for her symptoms and electrocardiographic changes. The entity, congenital left ventricle diverticulum, is reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
